  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA nor'easter centered south of Long Island drifts northwestward\nand then westward towards Long Island and New Jersey today. \nAfter reaching peak intensity, the low gradually fills in and \nweakens as it slowly move across southern New England through \nTuesday.\n\nStorm surge values of 1.0 to 1.5 feet remain on track. While \ntotal water level forecasts come up just short of minor flood \nstage, the Coastal Flood Advisory remains for large wave action.\nLarge near shore waves will bring ample splash over that will \nbring debris onto vulnerable coastal roadways through at least \ntonight's high tide cycle. In addition, dune erosion is expected\nand the most vulnerable locations will see the potential for \nsome inundation.\n\nHazardous conditions at the beaches and over the waters \ncontinue through at least Sunday night. Near shore waves of 10 \nto 15 feet continue, with only a gradual decrease in height late\ntoday through Monday. The High Surf Advisory that runs through \nSunday remains unchanged to cover the hazardous conditions from \nlarge waves and strong rip currents. It is important to note \nthat the long duration of a long fetch will create opportunities\nfor sneaker waves that will be larger embedded waves in a set \nand have potential to run farther into shore. Therefore, extra \ncaution is encouraged for those venturing to the beach to \nobserved the surf and avoid rock outcrops and jetties. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThe well advertised nor'easter will continue to drift north for\na little bit longer before stalling out somewhere off the coast\nof New Jersey by early today. The breadth of the storm is\nwidening some and this is finally allowing our area getting \ninto much needed rainfall. Bands of showers and steady rain, \nsome heavy at times will continue to rotate onshore today with a\nrumble of thunder not completely out of the question. The most \nbeneficial rainfall is expected across southern NH and coastal \nMaine. Further inland, the dry high pressure system to the north\nwill occasionally ridge southward which will limit rainfall \namounts, especially this afternoon and evening.\n\nAs far as winds go over land, we continue without any headlines.\nA few gusts on the coast and southeastern NH could reach 40 to\n45 MPH today, but the low level jet will begin a weakening\nprocess by later this afternoon, so peak SFC winds should \ndecrease by this evening into Monday.\n\nAreas south of the mountains and along the coast should see a \nwidespread 1 to 2 inches of rain while the latest HREF guidance \nsuggests a potential for higher amounts over far southeast New \nHampshire and far southwest Maine. Here, localized totals up to \n4 inches will be possible through Monday. CAMs suggest that \nprecipitation will likely come in waves with a convective \ncomponent. Therefore, a stray bolt of lightning may not be \nimpossible along the coast and over the waters. Embedded \ndownpours will also be possible with P-wats in the 90th \npercentile or greater for this time of year. While most of the \nprecipitation will start to taper off later in the day on \nMonday, the upper low will move overhead on Tuesday which may \nlead to some lingering areas of light rain. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAfter the nor'easter moves offshore, the 500mb pattern looks to\nfavor the development of a ridge over the eastern US. With high\npressure setting up south of New England, moderating \ntemperatures are expected for mid to late week with mainly dry \nconditions. Some very weak instability could allow for a stray \nshower to generate on Wednesday or Thursday, but most people \nshould stay dry. \n\nRain chances are a little higher on Friday, as a cold front is \npoised to move southward across New England. However rain \nchances are likely to remain in the slight-chance category, as \nthe front is very weak and any instability will have to overcome\na modest subsidence layer.",
